 ### Monitoring implications: the next-best measurement per dataset
 
-Because the tier-ablation and external-transfer results isolate strontium and silica as the specific measurements whose absence drives Lower Anayari's near-total non-identifiability and a substantial share of Northern Ghana's tier-3-to-tier-2 collapse, the practical implication is concrete rather than generic: for a silicate-dominated, data-limited Ghanaian aquifer, adding strontium and silica to an otherwise standard major-ion-plus-isotope panel is expected to yield a disproportionate reduction in ambiguity relative to adding further wells at the existing panel. Strontium and silica have long been used as discriminants between silicate- and carbonate-derived solutes in hydrogeochemistry; the contribution here is not the discovery that these tracers matter, which is established domain knowledge, but a dataset-specific, quantified demonstration of how much identifiability is lost when they are absent and a transferable illustration of how the same tier-ablation logic could be applied to price the value of any other candidate measurement. Fluoride mobilisation, a documented regional concern given widespread geogenic fluoride occurrence in Ghanaian and wider West African groundwater [@podgorski2022fluoride; @onipe2020fluoride], and agro-domestic nitrate loading [@tredoux2006nitrate] both remained majors-corroborable and comparatively stable across tiers in this study, so the measurement-value argument here is specific to weathering-family resolution and should not be generalised to every process class without re-running the same tier-ablation logic for the process of interest.
